  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Nkole\"\nsubtitle: \"Uganda\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Nkole, also called Banyankole, a Bantu people of south-western Uganda |\n| Where | Ankole region and surrounding districts (Mbarara, Bushenyi, Ntungamo, Kiruhura, Ibanda, Isingiro, Rukungiri, Buhweju, Mitooma, Rwampara, Sheema, Rubirizi, and parts of Kitagwenda) |\n| How many | Population not given in sources |\n| Language | Nkore (also written Nkole, Nyankore, Orunyankore, Runyankore, Runyankole) |\n| Religion | Predominantly Christian: 52.6% Anglican, 34.8% Roman Catholic, 5.2% Muslim, 4.6% Pentecostal |\n| Known for | Cattle-keeping and milk vessels · Coiled grass basketry with geometric patterns · Wooden weapons and tools · Glass beadwork and cowrie shell adornment |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Nkole are a Bantu people of south-western Uganda, closely related to the Nyoro, Kiga, Toro and Hema. They are known above all as cattle-keepers, and their material culture — milk vessels, baskets, weapons and tools — follows directly from that practice. Their objects show fine craftsmanship in wood, grass, iron and beadwork, organized around pale grounds with dark geometric patterns.\n\n## Material culture\n\nNkole material culture centers on basketry, woodworking, and ironworking, with a consistent aesthetic of pale grounds decorated with dark geometric figures.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nThe Nkole have no woven cloth tradition; their surface pattern lives in coiled grass basketry.\n\n- **Coiled grass basketry** — Baskets, bowls, plates, trays and covers are coiled from grass or leaf with black patterns on pale grounds, some shaped like beehives.\n- **Conical coiled covers** — Covers and plates in grass or leaf are coiled conically and patterned in black against pale, including fine lids on wooden milk vessels.\n- **Coloured banded coil-work** — Bottles and lids are coiled from dyed grass in red, black and yellow horizontal bands.\n- **Plaited grass matting** — Composite mats made of grass strands joined by transverse bands of black grass, sometimes with cane embroidered decoration.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe sources say almost nothing about Nkole dress.\n\n- **Head-dress mat** — A composite grass mat catalogued as both mat and head-dress, red and yellow with bands of cane embroidered decoration in dark grey and three fibre cords for attachment.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nIronworking and woodworking dominate Nkole material culture, centered on weapons, tools and milk vessels.\n\n- **Milk vessel** — A conical wooden vessel with a broad circular base, tapering neck and narrow aperture, topped with a finely coiled grass lid.\n- **Composite milkpot** — A wooden milkpot with a groove below the neck and iron and copper symbols stapled onto it.\n- **Bamboo quiver** — Quivers of bamboo, wood or hide carry arrows and are closed with hide or removable wooden caps, with wooden ones carved and painted in black.\n- **Iron spear** — A socketed iron spear blade on a hard wooden shaft with a short iron butt.\n- **Smoking pipe** — A pottery bowl paired with a wooden stem.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nGlass beads and cowrie shells are the main adornment materials, appearing on objects rather than worn on the body.\n\n- **Beaded fly-whisk** — A fibre and hair fly-whisk with a handle covered in bands of turquoise-blue, red, pink, white and black glass beads, cowries at the top, and cowries, a metal bell and wooden charms at the base.\n- **Beaded gourd bottle** — A gourd bottle entirely covered in a geometric design of dark blue, pink and white beads.\n- **Horn amulet** — An amulet made of horn, possibly for ritual use.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nThe sources name only one musical instrument and no song genres, ensembles or performance occasions.\n\n- **Musical horn** — A pale green spiral hollow horn with an oval hole near the tip.\n- **Ebyevugo** — A Runyankore poetry form, mentioned in sources but with no description of how it is performed.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nThe sources document no Nkole dances or dramatic traditions.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources describe no annual festivals or birth, initiation, marriage or funeral rites.\n\n- **Ritual fly-whisk** — A fly-whisk whose handle carries cowries, a metal bell and two wooden charms, associated with ritual use.\n\n## Foodways\n\nCattle-keeping is the foundation of Nkole foodways, and milk vessels reflect this central practice.\n\n- **Milk** — Milk is the primary food product, served in specialized wooden vessels.\n- **Matooke** (*ebitookye*) — Bananas or matooke, an everyday staple food.\n- **Beans** (*ebihimba*) — Beans, an everyday staple food.\n- **Millet bread** (*oburo*) — Millet bread, an everyday staple food.\n- **Maize** (*ebicoori*) — Maize or corn, eaten as maize meal or corn bread.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nThe sources give greeting formulas and one poetry form, but no folktales, epics, proverbs or riddles.\n\n- **Ebyevugo** — A Runyankore poetry form mentioned in the sources but with no description or named works.\n- **Greeting formula** (*Agandi*) — A greeting meaning literally 'other news!' and used for 'How are you?', answered with Ni marungi, 'good news!'.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nNkore is a Bantu language with high mutual intelligibility with Kiga; the Banyankole are predominantly Christian.\n\n- **Nkore language** (*Nkore / Runyankore*) — A Bantu language written variously as Nkole, Nyankore, Orunyankore, Runyankore and Runyankole, with 84–94% lexical similarity to Kiga.\n- **Address term for woman** (*Nyabo*) — The Runyankore word for addressing a woman.\n- **Address term for man** (*Sebo*) — The Runyankore word for addressing a man.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Nkore* — The Bantu language spoken by the Banyankole\n- *Runyankore* — Alternate spelling of Nkore language\n- *Banyankole* — The Nkole people\n- *Ankole* — The historical kingdom and region in south-western Uganda where the Nkole are native\n- *Ebyevugo* — A Runyankore poetry form\n- *Agandi* — Greeting meaning 'other news!' or 'How are you?'\n- *Ni marungi* — Response greeting meaning 'good news!'\n- *Nyabo* — Runyankore address term for a woman\n- *Sebo* — Runyankore address term for a man\n- *ebitookye* — Bananas or matooke in Runyankore\n- *ebihimba* — Beans in Runyankore\n- *oburo* — Millet bread in Runyankore\n- *ebicoori* — Maize in Runyankore\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- John Roscoe, *The Banyankole: the second part of the report of the Mackie Ethnological Expedition to Central Africa*, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1923, 176 p.\n- Charles V. Taylor, description and teaching guide to Nkore-Kiga (1950s), cited in the Wikipedia article on the Nkole language\n- \"Nkole (peuple d'Afrique)\", LCSH notice, Bibliothèque nationale de France\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nkole_people\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nkole_language\n- British Museum online collection: https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n- Smithsonian Folkways search: https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Uganda\n",
